      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Examine the association of medication adherence with workplace productivity and health-related quality of life (HRQL) in asthma patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Adult patients with asthma in a state health insurance program identified from medical claims (July 2001-June 2003) were mailed a three-part survey to measure HRQL (St. George's Respiratory Questionnaire), workplace productivity (Workplace Productivity Short Inventory), and self-reported medication adherence (Morisky Scale)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The symptoms domain had the worst HRQL scores, followed by the activity and impacts domains; 39% of the participants reported themselves as \"high\" adherent, whereas 19% were \"medium,\" and 42% were \"low\" adherent. Asthma resulted in productivity losses of 597 +/- 1,024 US dollars (absenteeism) and 658 +/- 1,808 US dollars (presenteeism) per enrollee per year."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Asthma was associated with HRQL detriments and workplace productivity losses."
        }
      ]},
